<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<!--
  Warrior Hotkey Bridge - per-user MSI.

  Built by installer/Build-Installer.ps1, which supplies ProductVersion and PublishDir.
  Authored against the WiX v4 schema namespace, which is shared unchanged by WiX v4, v5, v6
  and v7 - so this file compiles on any of them. The build pins v5.0.2 because v6 and v7
  require accepting the Open Source Maintenance Fee EULA and v5 does not.
-->

<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs">

  <!--
    Scope="perUser" is a requirement, not a preference. The bridge registers global hotkeys for
    one interactive session, writes its configuration and logs under %LOCALAPPDATA%, and starts
    with Windows through HKCU\...\Run. Every one of those is per-user state, so a per-machine
    install would put the binaries somewhere a second user could run them while all the state
    they depend on stayed with the first. Installing per-user also means no UAC prompt.
  -->
  <Package
      Name="Warrior Hotkey Bridge"
      Manufacturer="Patrick Miron"
      Version="$(var.ProductVersion)"
      UpgradeCode="5D55C5D4-7B52-42AF-9CE4-7819B8DEF9DE"
      Scope="perUser"
      Compressed="yes"
      InstallerVersion="500"
      Language="1033">

    <!--
      Schedule="afterInstallValidate" removes the previous version BEFORE the new files are
      copied. This is a deliberate choice between two real failure modes, and it is not the one a
      review picks on first reading - so the reasoning is recorded here rather than rediscovered.

      Removing LAST is the rollback-safe ordering. The old product stays registered until the new
      files are down, so a copy that fails partway can be rolled back to a working installation
      instead of leaving the machine with no executable and no Add/Remove Programs entry. That
      argument is correct as far as it goes, and this package was built that way for a while.

      It is nonetheless the wrong choice here, because of how Windows Installer decides whether to
      copy a file at all. For a VERSIONED file - which every managed assembly is - the decision
      compares file versions, never contents. With the old files still present, a
      WarriorHotkeyBridge.dll carrying 1.0.0.0 does not replace a WarriorHotkeyBridge.dll carrying
      1.0.0.0, so the installer copies nothing and reports success. Measured rather than reasoned
      about: an install that returned exit code 0 left a managed assembly nine hours stale, and the
      feature it was meant to deliver was simply absent from the running process. File hashes do
      not rescue this; MSI consults them only for unversioned files.

      So the two orderings each guarantee one property and forfeit the other. Remove-last keeps a
      recoverable installation at every instant but cannot promise the new files are written.
      Remove-first guarantees the files are written - the destination is empty, so there is
      nothing to compare against - but has a window where neither version is present.

      Remove-first wins because of how the two fail. A failed upgrade is rare, immediately
      visible, and repaired by running the installer again. A skipped copy is silent, reports
      success, and leaves the operator running code they believe was replaced. For an application
      that places trades, that is not an acceptable failure mode.

      The residual risk is real and worth stating plainly: if the copy fails after the old product
      has gone, the machine is left with neither version until the installer is run again.

      The way to have both properties is to make every build carry a distinct FILE version, so
      remove-last can compare versions meaningfully. That is a build-stamping change, not an
      installer one, and it is deliberately not done here: a scheme that silently degrades to this
      same bug the first time someone forgets to bump a number is worse than an ordering that
      cannot degrade at all.

      None of this touches %LOCALAPPDATA%\WarriorHotkeyBridge. Configuration, logs, the startup
      preference and the Chrome profile are not MSI-managed resources, so the removal step leaves
      them alone and they are still there when the new version starts.
    -->

    <!--
      AllowSameVersionUpgrades is not optional here, despite what its name suggests.

      Without it the upgrade range stops just short of the current version, so installing 1.0.0
      over an existing 1.0.0 is not recognised as an upgrade at all - Windows Installer treats
      the rebuilt package as an unrelated product and installs it alongside, leaving two entries
      in Add/Remove Programs that share one directory. Reinstalling the same version to repair a
      bad install is an ordinary thing to do, and a rebuild always carries a fresh ProductCode,
      so this is reachable in normal use rather than only in testing.

      It is also what makes the pre-install stop reliable: an unrecognised install sets neither
      Installed nor WIX_UPGRADE_DETECTED, so anything conditioned on those would be skipped
      while a running instance still held its files.
    -->
    <MajorUpgrade
        AllowSameVersionUpgrades="yes"
        Schedule="afterInstallValidate"
        DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of [ProductName] is already installed. Uninstall it first if you want to go back to this version." />

    <!--
      EmbedCab keeps the product a single .msi file. The payload is ~210 MB, almost half of it
      the Node runtime that Playwright's .NET client drives the browser through, so high
      compression is worth the extra build time.
    -->
    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" CompressionLevel="high" />

    <!-- No system restore point for a per-user tray utility that writes nothing outside HKCU. -->
    <Property Id="MSIFASTINSTALL" Value="1" />

    <!--
      This installer must never restart the machine, under any circumstances.

      It writes nothing outside the user's own profile and touches no system file, so a reboot
      could never be legitimately required - the only way one gets requested is a file left in
      use, and the honest response to that is to fail visibly and let the operator retry. A
      silent install that hits a locked file will otherwise restart Windows on its own, which for
      a tool used during market hours is a far worse outcome than a failed install. Observed
      exactly once, the hard way: msiexec returned 1641 and took the machine down with it.
    -->
    <Property Id="REBOOT" Value="ReallySuppress" />

    <!-- There is nothing to reconfigure; the tray icon and appsettings.json are the controls. -->
    <Property Id="ARPNOMODIFY" Value="1" />
    <Property Id="ARPHELPLINK" Value="https://github.com/" />

    <Feature Id="Main" Title="Warrior Hotkey Bridge" Level="1">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="ProductFiles" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="ProductShortcuts" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="DeckShortcuts" />
    </Feature>

    <!--
      %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs is where per-user applications belong on modern Windows; it is
      what a per-user Chrome, VS Code or Teams install uses. Program Files is not writable
      without elevation, which is the whole reason a per-user install exists.
    -->
    <StandardDirectory Id="LocalAppDataFolder">
      <Directory Id="ProgramsFolder" Name="Programs">
        <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="WarriorHotkeyBridge" />
      </Directory>

      <!--
        The operator's data folder. The installer creates nothing in it except the Deck
        subdirectory below - configuration, logs, the startup preference and the Chrome profile
        are all written by the application and must survive uninstall, so they are deliberately
        not MSI resources.
      -->
      <Directory Id="UserDataFolder" Name="WarriorHotkeyBridge">
        <Directory Id="DECKFOLDER" Name="Deck" />
      </Directory>
    </StandardDirectory>

    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramMenuFolder">
      <Directory Id="ShortcutFolder" Name="Warrior Hotkey Bridge" />
    </StandardDirectory>

    <!--
      Harvested from the publish output rather than listed by hand. A self-contained .NET
      publish is hundreds of files that change with every SDK update; enumerating them here
      would guarantee an installer that silently ships an incomplete runtime one day.
    -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="ProductFiles" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <Files Include="$(var.PublishDir)\**" />
    </ComponentGroup>

    <!--
      Shortcuts for a Stream Deck to launch.

      They exist separately from the Start Menu entries for two reasons. They pass the silent
      switch, because a deck button that is pressed twice must not raise an "already running"
      dialog - pressing an already-pressed button is a normal thing to do, and the right answer
      is to carry on quietly. And they live at a path the operator binds once: deck software
      stores the path it was given, so the files have to reappear at exactly the same place after
      every upgrade or the buttons quietly stop working.

      That path is under the operator's data folder rather than the install folder. Both are
      stable, but this one reads as belonging to them, and it keeps the deck bindings away from
      the directory whose entire contents are replaced on upgrade.
    -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="DeckShortcuts">
      <Component Id="StreamDeckShortcuts" Directory="DECKFOLDER" Guid="926C2318-0D46-4794-B2B9-21E756114950">

        <Shortcut
            Id="DeckGoShortcut"
            Name="Go Trading"
            Description="Start the hotkey bridge and its Chrome, and claim F13-F24."
            Target="[INSTALLFOLDER]WarriorHotkeyBridge.exe"
            Arguments="--silent"
            WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER" />

        <Shortcut
            Id="DeckStopShortcut"
            Name="Stop Trading"
            Description="Stop the bridge, release F13-F24 to other applications, and close its Chrome."
            Target="[INSTALLFOLDER]WarriorHotkeyBridge.exe"
            Arguments="--quit --close-chrome --silent"
            WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER" />

        <!--
          Removes the Deck directory only. The parent holds configuration, logs and the Chrome
          profile, none of which this installer created or may delete.
        -->
        <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveDeckFolder" Directory="DECKFOLDER" On="uninstall" />

        <RegistryValue
            Root="HKCU"
            Key="Software\WarriorHotkeyBridge\Installer"
            Name="DeckShortcuts"
            Type="integer"
            Value="1"
            KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>

    <ComponentGroup Id="ProductShortcuts">
      <Component Id="StartMenuShortcuts" Directory="ShortcutFolder" Guid="3E0E7913-07DC-4EA2-B883-0382D83C6212">

        <Shortcut
            Id="LaunchShortcut"
            Name="Warrior Hotkey Bridge"
            Description="Start the hotkey bridge in the notification area."
            Target="[INSTALLFOLDER]WarriorHotkeyBridge.exe"
            WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER" />

        <Shortcut
            Id="LaunchDebugShortcut"
            Name="Warrior Hotkey Bridge (Debug Console)"
            Description="Start with a diagnostic console window and Debug-level logging."
            Target="[INSTALLFOLDER]WarriorHotkeyBridge.exe"
            Arguments="--debug"
            WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER" />

        <!--
          The counterpart to the Stream Deck stop button, for when the deck is not to hand.
          Closes the dedicated Chrome instance too, which is what releases the hotkeys back to
          other applications.
        -->
        <Shortcut
            Id="StopShortcut"
            Name="Stop Warrior Hotkey Bridge"
            Description="Ask a running bridge to exit and close its Chrome instance."
            Target="[INSTALLFOLDER]WarriorHotkeyBridge.exe"
            Arguments="--quit --close-chrome"
            WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER" />

        <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveShortcutFolder" Directory="ShortcutFolder" On="uninstall" />

        <!--
          A per-user component needs an HKCU keypath; a file keypath under a user profile is
          what ICE38/ICE64 flag and what makes repair unreliable for per-user installs.
        -->
        <RegistryValue
            Root="HKCU"
            Key="Software\WarriorHotkeyBridge\Installer"
            Name="Shortcuts"
            Type="integer"
            Value="1"
            KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>

    <!--
      Stop a running bridge before touching its files.

      Without this an upgrade over a running instance hits MSI's files-in-use path: either a
      dialog asking the operator to close an application that has no window, or a queued reboot.
      The quit switch now blocks until the instance has released its single-instance mutex, so
      when this action returns the executable is genuinely unlocked.

      Return="ignore" because every failure mode here is benign - no previous version, no
      running instance, or a stop that timed out - and none of them justify failing an install.

      The silent switch matters more than it looks. Without it the executable allocates a console
      window when there is no terminal to attach to, so an unattended install would put a black
      window on screen for as long as the stop took, and a stop that timed out would raise a
      dialog with nobody present to dismiss it.
    -->
    <CustomAction
        Id="StopRunningBridge"
        Directory="INSTALLFOLDER"
        ExeCommand="&quot;[INSTALLFOLDER]WarriorHotkeyBridge.exe&quot; --quit --silent"
        Execute="immediate"
        Impersonate="yes"
        Return="ignore" />

    <!--
      Remove the Run registration on uninstall by asking the application to do it.

      The MSI deliberately does not declare that registry value as a resource. An MSI owns what
      it installs, so a declared Run value would be rewritten by every repair and every upgrade,
      overriding an operator who had switched startup off - the opposite of "upgrades preserve
      preferences". Ownership stays with the application; the uninstaller just asks it to clean up.

      Scheduled before RemoveFiles because it runs the very executable that is about to be
      deleted, and conditioned so it fires on a real uninstall only: during a major upgrade the
      old product is removed with UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE set, and clearing startup there would
      silently disable it for anyone who had it on.
    -->
    <CustomAction
        Id="RemoveStartupRegistration"
        Directory="INSTALLFOLDER"
        ExeCommand="&quot;[INSTALLFOLDER]WarriorHotkeyBridge.exe&quot; --uninstall-cleanup --silent"
        Execute="immediate"
        Impersonate="yes"
        Return="ignore" />

    <!--
      Start the bridge once installation finishes.

      Without this the installer completes silently and nothing is running: no tray icon, no
      registered hotkeys, and no obvious signal that anything was installed at all. For a
      resident tray application with no main window that reads as a failed install, and for this
      one in particular the failure is invisible in the worst way - the keys simply do nothing.

      asyncNoWait because the process is meant to outlive the installer. Anything else would
      leave msiexec waiting for a tray application that only exits when the operator says so.
    -->
    <CustomAction
        Id="LaunchBridge"
        Directory="INSTALLFOLDER"
        ExeCommand="&quot;[INSTALLFOLDER]WarriorHotkeyBridge.exe&quot;"
        Execute="immediate"
        Impersonate="yes"
        Return="asyncNoWait" />

    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <!--
        Before InstallValidate, which is where MSI decides whether files are in use.

        Deliberately unconditional. An earlier version ran this only when Installed or
        WIX_UPGRADE_DETECTED was set, which sounds like a safe narrowing and is not: those are
        exactly the properties that are unset when a package is not recognised as an upgrade, so
        the one case that most needs a running instance stopped was the one case that skipped
        stopping it. On a genuine first install there is simply no executable at that path yet,
        the action fails to start anything, and Return="ignore" absorbs it.
      -->
      <Custom Action="StopRunningBridge" Before="InstallValidate" />

      <Custom Action="RemoveStartupRegistration" Before="RemoveFiles" Condition="REMOVE=&quot;ALL&quot; AND NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE" />

      <!--
        Everything except an uninstall. The pairing with StopRunningBridge is the whole point:
        that action runs unconditionally and stops whatever was running, so anything narrower
        here leaves a case where the bridge is stopped and never comes back. "NOT Installed"
        looked sufficient and was not - reinstalling the same package is a maintenance install of
        a ProductCode that is already present, so Installed is true, and the bridge was stopped
        and left down.

        The cost of being this broad is that repairing an installation starts the bridge even if
        it was not running beforehand. That is a fair trade: the operator has just deliberately
        run an installer, a tray application appearing is unsurprising and one menu click to
        undo, whereas the failure it prevents is silently dead trading hotkeys discovered at the
        worst possible moment.
      -->
      <Custom Action="LaunchBridge" After="InstallFinalize" Condition="NOT REMOVE=&quot;ALL&quot;" />
    </InstallExecuteSequence>

  </Package>
</Wix>
